tle was strange and wonderful: a feather of fire's light against the Shadow Crow's dark, Wren's courage against the Shadow Crow's spite, a talking fox's loyalty against everything. Light, it turns out, is very stubborn."}, {'n': 4, 'title': 'Starlight Restored', 'text': "the Enchanted Forest celebrated: the fireflies spelled Wren's name, the oak dropped acorns of thanks, the wise owl composed a ballad with a talking fox as the hero — a talking fox insisted Wren was the hero; they compromised on both.\n\na feather of fire chose to stay with Wren — or Wren chose to stay with it; by now they weren't sure there was a difference. It sits by the bed, glowing softly, a nightlight of pure story.\n\nThey carried the starlight home to the Enchanted Forest in a feather of fire, which had grown to hold it all. The brook ran forward again. The mushrooms glowed gold. The stars came back, one by one, like friends returning."}, {'n': 5, 'title': 'The Promise Kept', 'text': "the Enchanted Forest thrives: the enchanted forest enchants, the glade whispers kindly, the meadow moonlights. All because one child picked up a feather of fire and one friend said 'together.'\n\nNew children come to hear the story, and Wren tells it true: the fear, the laughter, the moment it almost went wrong. 'Were you scared?' they ask. 'Terrified,' Wren says. 'That's what brave is.'\n\neven small friends are mighty. In the Enchanted Forest, in the starlight, in every heart that hears the tale: the promise kept, the story told, the magic real."}, {'n': 6, 'title': 'Bedtime', 'text': "Wren smiles from the doorway. Outside, the stars are out — all of them, every one, back where they belong. The brook sings. The fireflies dance.\n\nAnd now it's bedtime in the Enchanted Forest, and a feather of fire glows soft, and a talking fox is telling the story one more time, to one more sleepy listener. 'The end,' a talking fox says.\n\neven small friends are mighty. Goodnight."}]}

