earlessness.\n\nThe battle was strange and wonderful: a silver acorn's light against the Thorn Witch's dark, Sage's courage against the Thorn Witch's spite, a talking fox's loyalty against everything. Light, it turns out, is very stubborn."}, {'n': 4, 'title': 'Starlight Restored', 'text': "They carried the starlight home to the Moonlit Meadow in a silver acorn, which had grown to hold it all. The brook ran forward again. The mushrooms glowed gold. The stars came back, one by one, like friends returning.\n\nbe brave, be kind — Sage wrote it in the family book, under the date, in the careful letters of someone who means it forever.\n\na silver acorn chose to stay with Sage — or Sage chose to stay with it; by now they weren't sure there was a difference. It sits by the bed, glowing softly, a nightlight of pure story."}, {'n': 5, 'title': 'The Promise Kept', 'text': "be brave, be kind. In the Moonlit Meadow, in the starlight, in every heart that hears the tale: the promise kept, the story told, the magic real.\n\nthe Moonlit Meadow thrives: the enchanted forest enchants, the glade whispers kindly, the meadow moonlights. All because one child picked up a silver acorn and one friend said 'together.'\n\nSage grew, as heroes do, but never grew out of the Moonlit Meadow. Every spring they check the old oak. Every night a silver acorn glows. Every tale a talking fox tells starts, 'When we saved the stars...'"}, {'n': 6, 'title': 'Bedtime', 'text': "'Again,' says the listener. It is always 'again.' The best stories are.\n\nAnd now it's bedtime in the Moonlit Meadow, and a silver acorn glows soft, and a talking fox is telling the story one more time, to one more sleepy listener. 'The end,' a talking fox says.\n\nSleep tight, the Moonlit Meadow. The dark is just the blanket. The light is a silver acorn, and a silver acorn is here, and Sage is here, and a talking fox is here."}]}

